WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.010
Hi, I'm Alisha Brooks.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.870
And I'm Elizabeth Austin-Davis.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.230
And welcome to New Growth,
presented by Strong Black Lead.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.340
We are so excited to have you at The
Black Hair Experience in our DMV location

00:00:11.341 --> 00:00:16.050
as we have a conversation with
two powerhouse women who are

00:00:16.051 --> 00:00:18.480
navigating the ever-changing
Black hair space.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.040
We'll be joined by celebrity hairstylist,

00:00:20.041 --> 00:00:23.640
Cheryl Bergamy and Oscar
winning hairstylist, Mia Neal.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.440
I just think about when I first met
you during the time that we were in

00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:33.360
corporate America, trying to really figure
out how we were going to do our hair.

00:00:33.361 --> 00:00:37.860
It started out with relaxers. We dealt
with micro-aggressions in the workplace,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.470
feeling like we had to do things
to our hair to help us fit in.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.270
But because of our friendship,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.810
we were really able to navigate and create
that support system together and our

00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:47.070
decision to go natural.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.800
And that's why we're going to be
having conversations about our hair

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:53.550
experiences, about the Crown Act and
celebrating this year's Crown Day.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.430
So we're joined by New
York celebrity hairstylist,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.670
Cheryl Bergamy whose work has
been featured all over Broadway,

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.700
in print media and styling
some of your favorite celebs.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.440
We're also joined by Mia Neal,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:13.310
who's one of the first black woman
to win an Oscar for hairstyling.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.160
Her work has been featured
all over TV and media,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.220
and she's known for some
of her amazing wig work.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:22.880
Welcome ladies. Thank you
so much for being here.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.380
So I want to ask you Mia,

00:01:24.381 --> 00:01:28.100
to give us some background on how you got
to the Oscar. The journey before that.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:33.380
I was born and raised in Gary
Indiana. My aunt had a salon there...

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.990
Jackie's Hair To Dye For.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.050
I was a shampoo girl, but if you showed
up late, or if you made Jackie mad,

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:46.320
I became your stylist.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.210
And I was like 12 years old. I was like,

00:01:53.211 --> 00:01:54.860
I gonna do a French roll with a finger
wave standing up. And the person

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.440
is like 75 years old, right?
With the waterfalls coming down.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:07.460
Like I did all of these like really
elaborate hairstyles and, um, you know,

00:02:07.461 --> 00:02:08.750
that's how I like, started off.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.740
Love it. I love it.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:14.330
My first time interacting
with hair, I was around eight,

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.950
nine years old and my mother
was diagnosed with breast cancer

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:23.750
and she got to a point where
she was too weak to do my hair.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.410
So she said to me, Cheryl, I
can't do your hair anymore.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.420
You're going to have to
do your hair for yourself.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.870
And so the first thing
she taught me was braids.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:37.010
I learned how to braid on all my
dolls. Soon after, she passed away.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.630
And so, I saw myself doing
hair more. It relaxed me.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.640
It was a form of therapy for me.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.820
So I started doing hair at school,

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.630
the proms and it just like, kind
of like grew from there. You know?

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:55.100
Ultimately like, your passion is
your passion. You know what I mean?

00:02:55.101 --> 00:02:58.370
You're always going to be led back
to it. Now, I had no idea about like,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.980
film and TV and you know,

00:02:59.981 --> 00:03:03.280
having a pension and being a part of
a union and not working in a salon

00:03:03.281 --> 00:03:07.540
necessarily. So when people say, well,
you have this Oscar, it's like, no,

00:03:07.541 --> 00:03:11.980
that Oscar is everybody's Oscar. You
know, that Oscar belongs to all of us.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.930
It's like, everybody's put that work in.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.750
So all of this kind of makes me think
about Cicely Tyson and her just,

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.080
unapologetically being
herself on set. You know,

00:03:22.081 --> 00:03:23.610
it's crazy to think that in the sixties,

00:03:23.611 --> 00:03:27.810
she was the first black woman to rock
her natural hair. Who would you say now,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.830
in modern times,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.490
that you would think is influential when
it comes to rocking their natural hair?

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.210
I mean, I really love Viola
Davis. I mean, we all do right?

00:03:36.211 --> 00:03:40.500
That's how she rolls. She gives you
Afro. She gives you loose curls.

00:03:40.501 --> 00:03:41.850
Like the flexibility is there.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.930
I think for me, it's, Issa
Rae. Like, I am a huge fan.

00:03:45.931 --> 00:03:49.110
I love her energy and how
she rocks her hair. And like,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.580
what about Teyonah Parris? Like,

00:03:50.791 --> 00:03:53.850
seeing that representation in the Marvel
Universe. Come on, hands down, okay? I

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.560
never thought that I would see that
growing up, but I'm happy to see it here.

00:03:58.561 --> 00:04:01.680
Yes. And we can't wait to see
her in They Cloned Tyrone.

00:04:01.681 --> 00:04:02.760
I'm looking forward to that.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:03.450
Yes.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.540
Chloe and Halle. Yeah.
Those two girls. I mean,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.380
they absolutely wearing their
locs and it just look amazing.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.660
When I was in high school,
everybody was just, you know,

00:04:12.661 --> 00:04:14.310
worried about making sure
that their hair was straight.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.850
And nowadays, it's like, the youth
are like, so give me that purple,

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.320
can we add another inch to that nail?
I mean, that's the beauty right?

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:30.450
You can really do anything, right?
You know, like right now, you know,

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.110
I wear my hair straight. But I also have
had it in a big curly Afro, you know?

00:04:34.111 --> 00:04:37.320
I've had locs down to my waist. You know,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.540
my hair has like told
the story of my life.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:45.570
Like you could tell where I was
culturally all throughout that process,

00:04:45.571 --> 00:04:46.404
you know?

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.200
Okay, ladies. So we've been talking
about being our natural selves.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.840
And the first thing that came
to my mind is the Crown Act.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.820
And it was introduced in 2019.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:58.170
And it's all about us being able to wear
our hair as we please, where we please.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.060
Um, so how do you guys feel about that?

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.640
When I first heard about the Crown Act,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:07.740
I thought about my childhood and
my grandfather telling me that

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.670
I will be the first generation in
our family that would actually have

00:05:11.671 --> 00:05:15.450
legislation to back up
their rights. But hey,

00:05:15.451 --> 00:05:17.850
I'm all for some legislation.
Let's put it on the books.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.570
I would agree with that. And I think
for me, like, even in that journey of,

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:26.490
of going natural, like I really had
to learn the power of our hair. Like,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:30.600
an understanding that, oh, I could be
natural and I can be straight. Or, oh,

00:05:30.601 --> 00:05:32.430
I could be natural and I can have braids.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.190
So it took me some time to
like really understand like,

00:05:35.191 --> 00:05:39.720
what that was like for me to be able
to walk around with my natural hair.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:44.310
So, I want to spend some time talking
about our hair and how we were navigating

00:05:44.311 --> 00:05:47.280
through this pandemic. I know
for me, it was some trying times.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.780
I was having a love hate
relationship with my hair.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:56.370
I spent a lot of time in crochets,
a lot of time crying. Um,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.280
so can we talk little bit more about like,

00:05:58.281 --> 00:06:01.280
how that was for you guys during
the pandemic, as a stylist.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.510
When the Pandemic first started I think
we all thought it was going to be short.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:05.171
Yes.

00:06:05.171 --> 00:06:06.004
You know,

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:10.630
when it hit about like four or five
months and there seemed to be no end in

00:06:10.631 --> 00:06:14.140
sight, that was like the moment where
people were like, okay, hold up.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.040
I need you to explain to me what I can do.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.430
And that's when like a lot of my friends
started calling and especially guys.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:24.640
They were like, can you
talk me through a haircut?

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.940
A lot of people started to
like transition into, you know,

00:06:27.941 --> 00:06:29.800
being more self-sufficient,

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.430
taking that time to experiment on
something new that they wanted to do.

00:06:33.431 --> 00:06:36.880
Like let the grey grow to see
what you look like with that.

00:06:36.881 --> 00:06:39.400
And you could kind of do it in
the privacy of your own home.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.330
Letting the relaxers grow out.
Uh, trying something more natural.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:47.650
It had a hard moment for a while where
everybody was like, I just look crazy,

00:06:47.651 --> 00:06:52.180
you know? And then they're just like,
well, look what I did, you know?

00:06:52.181 --> 00:06:55.450
And then people like calling
me on Facetime. Girl, you
like this? And I'm like,

00:06:55.451 --> 00:06:56.800
yeah, that's cute! You know?

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.330
The self esteem around taking care
of themselves, like became greater,

00:07:01.331 --> 00:07:02.290
you know?

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:04.330
So, I think for me, like
going through the pandemic,

00:07:04.331 --> 00:07:06.730
I was totally wigging it
out the entire time. I mean,

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.620
you know, braiding my
own hair and, you know,

00:07:11.621 --> 00:07:15.430
just trying to protect it during that
time. Let it just be, you know? Like,

00:07:15.611 --> 00:07:18.190
it is okay to be in this state.
We're just gonna let it grow,

00:07:18.191 --> 00:07:20.230
no heat and just let it be in this moment.

00:07:20.231 --> 00:07:23.380
So that was definitely what I
was doing, learning and like,

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:25.450
taking even more time with
my daughter's hair. Like,

00:07:25.661 --> 00:07:29.110
really trying to learn how to
braid for outside braids, for her.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:30.003
Right. Right.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.390
You know? Even trying to learn... So
like, us even bonding over, you know,

00:07:37.391 --> 00:07:40.770
her hair and teaching her like, on
her wash days and things like that.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:45.070
It's still a journey for us and her to
be able to wash her own hair and things

00:07:45.071 --> 00:07:46.240
like that, because she's still small,

00:07:46.241 --> 00:07:49.010
but it's definitely something
that we did over the pandemic.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:52.570
This has been an amazing
conversation. And I think,

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.210
what better place to have it than
at The Black Hair Experience?

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.340
The Crown Day is here.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:01.600
And just like new growth is a sign of
healthy hair. Ladies, happy Crown Day!

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:02.666
Happy Crown Day!

